        "title": "Differentiating a Quotient Containing Other Functions"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "51bfc37c-6f17-5ee6-a48b-e69da0c893ac",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{2 e^{x}}{\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the original quotient is defined.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 e^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 e^{x})^{\\prime}\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 e^{x})\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{2 e^{x} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 e^{x} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\cos^2 x}\\\\ &=\\frac{2e^x(\\cos x+\\sin x)}{\\cos^2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
